<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Catherine of Hungary, Queen of Serbia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th–14th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Catherine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> was a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-king-of-england">King Stephen</a> V of Hungary and the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dragutin">Stefan Dragutin</a>, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. Through her, Dragutin received from her brother Ladislaus IV in 1284 the Banate of Macsó with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> and the lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usora">Usora</a> and Soli, which became the base of his realm in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrmia">Syrmia</a>. Their son Vladislav II briefly claimed Dragutin's lands after his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/catherine-of-hungary-queen-of-serbia.jpg" alt="Catherine of Hungary, Queen of Serbia" width="320" height="319" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gmihail at Serbian Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 rs · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Catherine_of_Hungary,_Queen_of_Serbia,_St._Achilleos_monastery,_Arilje,_1296.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
